WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.141
Validation occurs at several layers.

00:00:02.291 --> 00:00:07.957
Bean Validation checks request shape and ranges, domain methods enforce business invariants,

00:00:07.957 --> 00:00:11.734
and database constraints protect persisted state under concurrency.

00:00:11.884 --> 00:00:13.993
None completely replaces the others.

00:00:14.143 --> 00:00:18.545
A request may pass field validation but still conflict with current stock

00:00:18.545 --> 00:00:20.746
or a previously used idempotency key.

00:00:20.896 --> 00:00:26.569
Return stable error categories without leaking SQL, stack traces, or credentials.

00:00:26.719 --> 00:00:31.118
Problem Details gives an interoperable structure for HTTP errors.

00:00:31.268 --> 00:00:36.307
Include useful client information such as which field is invalid and a

00:00:36.307 --> 00:00:40.507
correlation identifier, while detailed internal diagnostics stay in protected logs.

00:00:40.657 --> 00:00:45.141
ReserveRequest requires a positive product ID and a quantity between one and

00:00:45.141 --> 00:00:45.889
one thousand.

00:00:46.039 --> 00:00:51.127
InventoryService rechecks its own boundary because it can also be called outside

00:00:51.127 --> 00:00:51.551
HTTP.

00:00:51.701 --> 00:00:54.658
A database check constraint prevents negative stock.

00:00:54.808 --> 00:01:01.298
Conflicts become 409; missing products become 404; invalid arguments become 400.

00:01:01.448 --> 00:01:06.231
Send malformed JSON, quantity zero, an unknown product, and a repeated key

00:01:06.231 --> 00:01:07.826
with a different quantity.

00:01:07.976 --> 00:01:12.373
Write down the expected status and verify it with the provided HTTP

00:01:12.373 --> 00:01:14.206
smoke test or Postman collection.

00:01:14.356 --> 00:01:19.932
Malformed or invalid input should return 400, unknown product 404, and a

00:01:19.932 --> 00:01:21.327
conflicting key 409.

00:01:21.477 --> 00:01:25.273
Confirm that failed requests do not alter stock or create orders.

00:01:25.423 --> 00:01:29.919
Avoid a catch-all handler that converts every programming defect into a misleading

00:01:29.919 --> 00:01:30.668
client error.

00:01:30.818 --> 00:01:31.489
Question.

00:01:31.639 --> 00:01:33.488
Why validate again in a service?

00:01:33.638 --> 00:01:34.214
Answer.

00:01:34.364 --> 00:01:39.596
Services may be invoked by jobs, tests, or other code that bypasses

00:01:39.596 --> 00:01:40.904
the HTTP validator.

00:01:41.054 --> 00:01:41.725
Question.

00:01:41.875 --> 00:01:43.828
Why keep database constraints?

00:01:43.978 --> 00:01:44.554
Answer.

00:01:44.704 --> 00:01:49.626
They enforce invariants at the durable shared boundary even when concurrent callers

00:01:49.626 --> 00:01:52.087
or another application bypass application checks.
